  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe area of low pressure that impacted the region yesterday will \ncontinue to move eastward, bringing drier weather through the \nafternoon with decreasing clouds expected from the coast to the \nfoothills into the evening. Relatively zonal/weakly offshore flow \ntakes over for the weekend, with temperatures remaining 5 to 10 \ndegrees below normal for areas away from the coast. This will \ninclude some chilly mornings across parts of the Inland Empire \nlowering into the 30s, high desert regions dipping below freezing, \nand areas around Big Bear Lake dropping into the teens. Winds will \nbe breeziest along the coastal slopes tonight out of the north and \neast with gusts mainly below 35 MPH.\n\nA stronger low pressure system is expected to churn off the Pacific \nNorthwest coastline over the weekend. A shortwave will break away, \npushing over SoCal by Sunday into early Monday. There has been a \nslight uptick in trends regarding precipitation amounts with this \nsystem; an increase in PWAT is also noted, though the atmosphere \nstill looks to be on the drier side. Chances for light precipitation \nare now in the forecast from Sunday evening/night for all areas \nexcept the lower deserts; some showers may linger into southern San \nDiego County (including the metro area) into Monday morning. The \nrain will be light, with general amounts near one tenth of inch, \nlocally higher in the mountains. Snow levels will be near 5,000 \nfeet, with communities in the San Bernardino Mts having the best \nchance to see accumulating snowfall. Snow amounts look to be \ngenerally under one inch for places like Big Bear Lake.\n\nModels are in fair agreement regarding another area of low pressure \nmoving closer to the Pacific Northwest by early next week. There \nstill remains some uncertainty in model guidance if a shortwave \nbreaks off from the system, similar to what the Sunday/Monday system \nwill do...or if the ridge to our south keeps the system north of our \nregion. If the system is able to move over our area, we will see a \nchance for light showers sometime Wednesday into Thursday morning \ncloser to the coast. Otherwise, dry weather will continue for many \nby the middle and end of next week as the ridge from the south \namplifies. This will bring a warming trend with highs 5 to 10 \ndegrees above normal.",
